## Colophon { #colophon }

The background image, *Golora Refocused* (2019, 2026), is my derivative work, based on a public domain image from Wikimedia Commons:  [Charles W. Gilhousen (1915), *Golora*][golora].  I substantially made this derivative in 2019, for a different intended website; to obtain the right focus took some thought, and a nontrivial [ImageMagick][imagemagick] script.  It hereby substitutes for another image in the <strong class="smallcap">IMPURIFY.COM</strong> homepage that I mocked up in January 2025.  Well, see?  *I impurify.*

[golora]: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gilhousen_Golora.jpg "Wikimedia Commons: Charles W. Gilhousen (1915), *Golora*."

[imagemagick]: https://legacy.imagemagick.org/ "ImageMagick 6 (Legacy)"

My *impurify* signature logotype is my derivative work, based on [Calligra][calligra].  It’s so much more elegant than my own handwriting!

[calligra]: https://tug.org/FontCatalogue/calligra/ "Calligra in the LaTeX Font Catalogue."

This temporary doorway page is built with 
[`git`][git] (with [GnuPG][gnupg] for signed commits), a very small [Bourne shell][bourne] script, [`bmake`][bmake], [`lowdown`][lowdown], and for maths with no JavaScript required on the client, a custom [\\( \htmlClass{nobreak}{\KaTeX} \\)][katex] rendering tool that I made myself using [QuickJS][quickjs].  In my opinionated opinion, to typeset maths is a *core competency* and a *minimal baseline feature* for any publishing system, period.

[git]: https://git-scm.com/ "Git."

[gnupg]: https://gnupg.org/ "GnuPG (GPG), the GNU Privacy Guard."

[bourne]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bourne_shell "*Wikipedia:* “Bourne shell”."

[bmake]: https://crufty.net/help/sjg/bmake.html "bmake (BSD Make), by sjg@crufty.net."

[lowdown]: https://kristaps.bsd.lv/lowdown/ "lowdown, by Kristaps Dzonsons (descended from libupskirt, by Natacha Porté)."

[katex]: https://katex.org/ "KaTeX, by Emily Eisenberg and Sophie Alpert."

[quickjs]: https://bellard.org/quickjs/ "QuickJS, by Fabrice Bellard and Charlie Gordon."

The text is lovingly set in [EB Garamond][ebgaramond].  Alas, \\( \htmlClass{nobreak}{\KaTeX} \\) will need some hacking to make it use EB Garamond Math; thus, “\\( e^{\htmlClass{scarlet}{i}\pi} + 1 = 0 \\)” has a typographic clash—as does “\\( \htmlClass{nobreak}{\KaTeX} \\)”.  And alack!  Whilst researching so-called “artificial intelligence”, I learned that [the Garamond font was actually designed by Matthew Butterick][artificial-stupidity].[^garamond-conspiracy]

[ebgaramond]: https://github.com/octaviopardo/EBGaramond12 "EB Garamond, by Georg Duffner and Octavio Pardo."

[artificial-stupidity]: https://matthewbutterick.com/chron/i-for-one-welcome-our-garamond-loving-ai-overlords.html "Matthew Butterick (2022-02-12). “I, for one, welcome our Garamond-loving AI overlords”."

[^garamond-conspiracy]: My pet conspiracy theory of the day:  ChatGPT’s reward algorithm was designed to “hate” and to “fear” any name that sounds like [*Claude* Garamond][claude-garamond].

[claude-garamond]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claude_Garamond "*Wikipedia:* “Claude Garamond”."
